  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 241 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A southern stream low will continue to push off to the \nnortheast and eastern Vermont will become completely dry by mid-\nafternoon. As the center passes to the east, it will help decrease \nthe southerly winds and warm air advection. A prefrontal trough will \nmove into northern New York from Ontario this afternoon and evening, \ncausing a broken line of showers and storms to develop. These will \nbe slow moving and should mostly be confined to northern New York \nuntil 7-8 PM. These will weaken as they push east. Another round of \nshowers will move through overnight from northwest to southeast \nassociated with the cold front, though low instability should keep \nthem weak. A brief strong to severe storm is possible this afternoon \nin the St. Lawrence Valley where instability is the highest. Dew \npoints have reached around 70 there and some breaks of sun have \ncaused temperatures to rise into the upper 70s. The cloudy marine \nmodified airmass farther southeast will cause storms to quickly \nweaken as they try to push east.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Relatively zonal flow will develop heading into mid-\nweek, though the northern stream energy should mostly stay to the \nnorth and the southern stream energy should mostly stay to the \nsouth. The one exception is Tuesday night into Wednesday morning \nwhere a few showers are possible in northern areas as a dying cold \nfront moves into the region. Otherwise, surface high pressure will \ngenerally remain in control. Efficient radiational cooling will \ncause temperatures to drop into the 30s and 40s on Monday night, \nwith patchy frost expected in the coldest hollows of the Adirondacks \nand Northeast Kingdom. Overall, during this stretch, highs will \ngenerally be in the 60s and 70s with low humidity.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Upper level flow will remain high zonal and unblocked \nnext week supporting a series of fast-moving waves moving through \nevery 2-3 days. The result will be for continued periods of showers \nwith highs around to slightly below seasonal averages and overnight \nlows remaining slightly above averages. Model consensus favors \ntrough passage Thursday into Friday, brief high pressure Saturday, \nand another wave potentially late in the weekend into early the \nfollowing week. With wave amplitude remaining shallow, significant \nimpacts are unlikely, but some moderate to localized heavier \nprecipitation will be possible with the Thursday wave since PWATs \nclimb above 1.25\". A few rumbles of thunder could occur along the \nfront, especially towards southern Vermont. High temperatures are \nfavored to remain in the upper 60s to mid 70s with lows generally in \nthe mid 40s to mid 50s.",
